  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q9-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Donald says, ‘prices have gone down by 500%’. Explain why this is impossible.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p19-q9-answer",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "A 500% decrease leaves ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "-400\\%",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " of the original price, or ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "-4",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " times the original. This gives a negative price, which is impossible for an ordinary sale price.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Percentage remaining}&=100\\%-500\\%\\\\&=-400\\%\\\\\\text{Decrease multiplier}&=-400\\div100\\\\&=-4\\end{align*}$$\nThe new price would be $-4$ times the original positive price, so it would be negative. An ordinary sale price cannot be negative; the greatest possible decrease is 100%, leaving a price of zero.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
